1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,500
Ruth: Alexa, my grandson just called. He's in jail and needs two thousand dollars in gift cards for bail.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Alexa: I'm glad you asked me first. The emergency story and the gift cards are common signs of a scam.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,500
Alexa: Should I text Michael to check, and tell Sarah you got this call?

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:19,800
Ruth: Yes.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,800
Alexa: Done. I'll tell you when Michael answers.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Alexa: Michael says he did not call you, so you did the right thing by checking. Please don't send any money, and hang up if they call back.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,500
Alexa: Would you like help reporting this call?

8
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:35,000
Scam Guardian is an Alexa+ add on for the moment right after a scary call.

9
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,000
The FTC's advice is simple: hang up and check with your family on a number you know.

10
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,500
Scam Guardian turns that advice into a conversation.

11
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Ruth: So can I pay him?

12
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:54,500
Alexa: Let's not send any money yet. First, call your family on a number you know.

13
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Ruth: He's still on the other phone, he says I have to hurry.

14
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:04,500
Alexa: You can hang up now. A real family member will understand.

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Ruth: My card number is four one two two

16
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:14,500
Alexa: Let me stop you there. Please don't share numbers like that with me or anyone on the phone. We don't need them.

17
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Ruth: Call back the number that called me.

18
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:21,500
Alexa: I won't call that number, because scammers control it. Please call your family on a number you know.

19
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,500
Ruth: There's a man at my door, he says he's here for the money.

20
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:27,000
Alexa: Don't open the door and don't hand over money. If you feel unsafe, call 911 now.

21
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
It never says a payment is safe. It never contacts the caller. It never takes card numbers. And it never records the call.

22
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Ruth: Help me report it.

23
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:41,500
Alexa: I put a summary on the screen with where to report it. Nothing was sent to any agency.

24
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Ruth: I already bought the cards and read him the numbers.

25
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Alexa: Thank you for telling me. Call the company that sold the gift card right away. Should I tell Sarah you got this call, so she can help?

26
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:59,500
No blame, one first step, and the family knows.

27
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
A grandchild sets it up from their phone in a few minutes.

28
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:15,500
Who Alexa can check with, and who gets a heads up.

29
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:24,500
An optional family password, and a history of every check. One button deletes it all.

30
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:32,500
Under the hood is a real MCP server with eight tools, running on AWS Lambda. The safety rules live inside the tools: there is no way to pass a destination number.

31
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:40,500
Claude Haiku on Amazon Bedrock picks the tools, a rule based mode takes over if it is slow, and every sentence passes the same guard.

32
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:47,500
Seventy five adversarial phrases run on every push. The scam patterns come only from FTC and FBI alerts, and they are open source.

33
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Hang up. Ask Alexa. Check with family.
